  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Welcome to CSP",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Welcome to CSP",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "creative_commons_license": "Creative Commons BY-NC-SA",
        "overview": "Welcome to Computer Science Principles! The first lesson is about getting students excited about the course and connecting their own personal interests to computer science.  Students are asked to share something they know a lot about and teach it to a small group.  Groups make a “rapid” prototype of an innovative idea and share it.  Students watch a brief video about computing innovations.  The lesson ends with students logging into the Code.org CSP course web site, and answering a brief prompt about what “computer science” means to them. This activity plants the initial seed for students to think about the ways in which they might be able to solve some problems relevant to their lives with technological innovations. ",
        "preparation": "- Procure poster paper for sharing innovations\n- Queue up CS is Changing Everything video\n- Setup section in Code Studio for this course\n- Have student sign-up link ready to share\n- Check the [\"Teacher's Lounge\"](https://forum.code.org/t/resource-hub-digital-information-lessons-1-11/36468) forum for verified teachers to find additional strategies or resources shared by fellow teachers",
        "student_overview": "Welcome to Computer Science Principles! Groups make a “rapid” prototype of an innovative idea and share it. Students watch a brief video about computing innovations.",
        "unplugged": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Welcome to CSP",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "csp-unit1-dev-27-28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Introduction to Apps",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Introduction to Apps",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "creative_commons_license": "Creative Commons BY-NC-SA",
        "overview": "Students explore and investigate what makes an app an app. They begin by looking at and discussing five different apps. Following this, students watch a video explaining the basics of how computers work. Finally students return to the apps and consider the various inputs and outputs.",
        "preparation": "* Preview the How Computers Work Video\n* Explore the apps used in this lesson\n- Check the [\"Teacher's Lounge\"](https://forum.code.org/t/resource-hub-intro-to-app-design-lessons-1-2-5-7/36472) forum for verified teachers to find additional strategies or resources shared by fellow teachers\n- If you are teaching virtually, consider checking our [Virtual Lesson Modifications](https://docs.google.com/document/d/146Dvs9RY0cvTDOaSWtxZAxBB3-c6yp993ap6gY_ocFQ/edit?usp=sharing)",
        "student_overview": "Welcome to the Introduction to App Development! We begin this unit by examining the basics of an app. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Introduction to Apps",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "csp-unit1-dev-27-28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Lesson 3:",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "csp-unit1-dev-27-28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: Representing Information",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Representing Information",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "creative_commons_license": "Creative Commons BY-NC-SA",
        "overview": "Using everyday materials, students create devices for sending information to a partner, which introduces the concept of sending bits of information from one place to another. Each group then uses its device to send an answer to a question. Following this, students modify their devices to answer more complex answers, responding with one of four possible messages, then one of eight possible messages, then one of sixteen possible messages. This lays the foundation for understanding how complex information is represented in computers using a combination of bits. ",
        "preparation": "- Reusable and consumable supplies for the classroom, such as markers, small flashlights, noisemakers, bells, whistles, cups and string, straws, slinkies, blocks, or colored paper.\n- Check the [\"Teacher's Lounge\"](https://forum.code.org/t/resource-hub-digital-information-lessons-1-11/36468) forum for verified teachers to find additional strategies or resources shared by fellow teachers\n- If you are teaching virtually, consider checking our [Virtual Lesson Modifications](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1iRhwURhXHSZqX1K_bis_A71IQVLalnrw8L1-9oQMMpk/edit?usp=sharing)",
        "student_overview": "Design a device for sending information across the room using common household supplies.",
        "unplugged": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: Representing Information",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "csp-unit1-dev-27-28"
      }
    }
  ],
