  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "c717d32f-3597-4bb6-b632-675c4a402558",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Throughout the year, in order for you to be able to see student work, and for students to be able to connect to each other on tools like the Internet Simulator, all students need to be in the same section on Code Studio. To see assessments and answer keys that Code.org provides, you also need to be a \"verified teacher\".",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**How-To Videos:** The following steps are also covered in a series of [r how_to_videos/csp-dev-2027/unversioned] available on Code.org's Teacher Support website. You may decide to watch these videos before reading the instructions below."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c717d32f-3597-4bb6-b632-675c4a402558",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cfa7edc-ebc6-402f-82b7-a1b1f8b258cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "097c4ea8-9318-43f3-b9de-51bbc2d0d07b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Do This:** Ensure you are registered on Code Studio as a \"verified\" teacher account\n\n* Anyone can create a teacher account on Code Studio, which means that we need an extra layer of authorization to allow CS Principles teachers to see assessments, answer keys, and any other collateral that students should not be able to trivially get access to. \n* If you attended a Code.org 5-day workshop during the summer, you should already have this access.\n* If not, you need to verify your teacher account. Please fill out [this form](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdGGAJuaDMBVIRYnimPhAL96w6fCl4UdvhwmynGONM75TWwWw/viewform). Note that it can take 5-7 business days to become a verified teacher, so please do this step early!\n\n**Do This:** Create a class section on Code Studio.\n\n![](https://images.code.org/7faaa833d7aea4ddbd943ed1f2bd0ed0-sec-setup 1.png)\n\n\n* You can either...\n\n\t* Go to the [r how_to_videos/csp-dev-2027/unversioned] for a video walkthrough of these steps and more on navigating your Code.org account.\n\n\t  Or...\n\n\t* Follow these steps to create a section:\n\n\t\t1. Navigate to the [My Dashboard](https://studio.code.org/home)\n\t\t2. Click 'New section' under 'Classroom Sections'\n\t\t3. Choose 'Email Logins' \n\t\t\t* If logged on through Google you can choose to sync to your Google Classroom.\n\t\t4. Give your section a name, and choose the most appropriate grade level of students in your class\n\t\t5. Set the course to be \"CS Principles\"\n\t\t6. Set the unit to be \"Unit 1: Digital Information\"\n\t\t7. *If you're NOT using Google Classroom:* Once the section is created, click the name of the section to show the unique \"join link\" for your section.\n",
        "name": "Before your first class\r"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "097c4ea8-9318-43f3-b9de-51bbc2d0d07b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cfa7edc-ebc6-402f-82b7-a1b1f8b258cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aae38c14-984b-435d-a4e1-e12cc53825eb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**NOTE: the following steps are only needed if you are not using Google Classroom** \n\n**Do This:**\n\n* Have students create a Code Studio account at [https://studio.code.org](https://studio.code.org) if they don't already have one\n\n[pull-right]\n\n![](https://images.code.org/13dc556bebe9bac66fdebbe3f0b05c36-image-1611867181623.png)\n\n[/pull-right]",
        "name": "Join Section\r"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aae38c14-984b-435d-a4e1-e12cc53825eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cfa7edc-ebc6-402f-82b7-a1b1f8b258cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c118da15-f99d-4b88-87f4-e327f9f8945a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  Share the section Join URL with students and tell them to navigate to it to join your section.  It will look something like what's shown at right.\n\n* You can confirm that a student successfully joined your section by having the section progress page on the Teacher Home Page open and hitting refresh as students join."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c118da15-f99d-4b88-87f4-e327f9f8945a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cfa7edc-ebc6-402f-82b7-a1b1f8b258cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "06287939-ced7-4263-bb93-f408d634bc6c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "* Students should also see a small green bar at the top of their page that says 'You've successfully joined ...'\n* Have students navigate to the course on the home page.\n* From the *student's* homepage at [studio.code.org](https://studio.code.org) they should see a \"tile\" for Computer Science Principles. Click 'View course', then go to Unit 1.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The slide icon <i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> indicates that there is a slide in the unit slide deck that corresponds to this activity or prompt. \n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "06287939-ced7-4263-bb93-f408d634bc6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cfa7edc-ebc6-402f-82b7-a1b1f8b258cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f46f026-e320-4fe4-a957-7b0a261b06ab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "- Welcome to Computer Science Principles! This year we’re going to learn a lot about computer science, but before we begin, we want to learn a little bit about you and your thoughts on computer science in general.",
        "name": "Teacher Message\r",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f46f026-e320-4fe4-a957-7b0a261b06ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d81d8e1f-24e4-4b1d-ae2f-a6f3dceae422"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a24b1074-a7c3-4d33-89fb-d3ca6e13a95f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One thing that makes this class unique is that you have to invent solutions to problems and create things all the time, both alone and with others. Everyone has a unique and creative perspective they bring to the table.\r\n    \r\nLet’s start by seeing how creative we can be right now!",
        "name": "Topic Brainstorm\r",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a24b1074-a7c3-4d33-89fb-d3ca6e13a95f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d81d8e1f-24e4-4b1d-ae2f-a6f3dceae422"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5615330d-e94e-4a24-a59d-ca541243cf62",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Do This:** Take out a journal or a piece of paper."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5615330d-e94e-4a24-a59d-ca541243cf62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d81d8e1f-24e4-4b1d-ae2f-a6f3dceae422"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6f3cd83-0f36-4a43-9cd7-702b79156518",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students need help coming up with ideas, share these tips:\n\n* This doesn’t have to be a subject in school - it very well might not be.  \n\n * As a person, as an individual who is living and breathing in this world, there is something that you probably know a lot about - maybe you feel like you know more about than most people. What is that thing?\n\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6f3cd83-0f36-4a43-9cd7-702b79156518",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d81d8e1f-24e4-4b1d-ae2f-a6f3dceae422"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7a74147-bae8-4749-a5fa-0508c8e4c90c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  **Prompt:** What’s something that you know a lot about? Something that you could teach somebody?\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7a74147-bae8-4749-a5fa-0508c8e4c90c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d81d8e1f-24e4-4b1d-ae2f-a6f3dceae422"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bce72ca6-910e-44dd-aa24-55e52ac2bbc3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "What you’re trying to do here is get students to state something that they are interested in, but also know a lot about - something they might have insights into.  \r\n\r\nA big part of students’ enthusiasm for sharing will come from your enthusiasm and genuine interest in getting to know them.  \r\n\r\nStudents might need prodding: there is something that makes them interesting and unique.  Something they like to do, have interest in, read about, have some expertise in, a hidden talent.\r\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bce72ca6-910e-44dd-aa24-55e52ac2bbc3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d81d8e1f-24e4-4b1d-ae2f-a6f3dceae422"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c38f2efb-85ea-4674-9612-7d5ab95f0287",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": " **Discuss:** With a partner or a small group:\r\n\r"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c38f2efb-85ea-4674-9612-7d5ab95f0287",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d81d8e1f-24e4-4b1d-ae2f-a6f3dceae422"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "155451b1-ab20-4cf6-85df-ceb4f10d569d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "* introduce yourself\r\n* explain the thing you know a lot about\r\n* tell the group something interesting about that topic"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "155451b1-ab20-4cf6-85df-ceb4f10d569d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d81d8e1f-24e4-4b1d-ae2f-a6f3dceae422"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bc97624-8f5c-4d9a-ba3d-871e1c814164",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Group:** Place students in small groups."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bc97624-8f5c-4d9a-ba3d-871e1c814164",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1108ecf7-4bcd-4f5f-af15-ad2ae6c1f06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d419a183-7d0e-4e08-a3b7-b64ce4c0aa98",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "People seem to say that technology is all around us, that it affects everything we do.  Is that true?  Technological [v innovation/csp/2021] is about recognizing a problem that needs to be solved, or recognizing something needs improving and then building a tool to solve it.\r\n\r\n**As a class we’re going to see how innovative we can be.  We’re going to do something called “rapid prototyping.”**  \r\n\r\n\"Prototype\" is a fancy word that means a simple sketch of an idea or model for something new.  It’s the original drawing from which something real might be built or created.",
        "name": "Identify impacts and prototype an innovation\r",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d419a183-7d0e-4e08-a3b7-b64ce4c0aa98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1108ecf7-4bcd-4f5f-af15-ad2ae6c1f06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1de4338-b124-4f39-9483-0e7d371fd0f7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Go around the group, and for each individual's area of interest:\r\n\r\n1. Identify some way that technology is used with, or affects that thing\r\n\r\n2. Make a suggestion for either:\r\n\r\n\t* a way that technology might be improved to make it better, faster, easier to use \r\n\t* a creative or innovative new technology that might help solve some problem within that area, or at least make better\r\n\r\nEveryone in the group should make suggestions for any of the areas of interest in your group.",
        "name": "Brainstorm Technological Innovation\r"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1de4338-b124-4f39-9483-0e7d371fd0f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1108ecf7-4bcd-4f5f-af15-ad2ae6c1f06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5cdbc1ef-505c-4075-846f-9326879db040",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep things quick.  If a group is worried about not being innovative enough, remind them that very small ideas can have big consequences.  People once thought it was ridiculous that you would want to send a short text message to another person over a phone.  \r\n\r\nAlternatively, a group may have a great idea that they want to spend more time on.  They can do that later.  For now, just remind them it’s a rapid prototype.\r\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5cdbc1ef-505c-4075-846f-9326879db040",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1108ecf7-4bcd-4f5f-af15-ad2ae6c1f06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83f4f4f5-8cf7-4190-992a-5690d5708245",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As a group you have just brainstormed about the technology ideas at the table.  \n\nNow, come together and get excited about **one** of them. \n\nAs a group, nominate the idea you’ve discussed that you think would be the *most interesting* to everyone else in the class. \n\n**Start to sketch out that idea on a poster.  Make a visual representation of your ideas.**  \n\n * Remember this is a rapid prototype.  Just something to quickly convey the idea.",
        "name": "Rapid Prototype one idea\r",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83f4f4f5-8cf7-4190-992a-5690d5708245",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1108ecf7-4bcd-4f5f-af15-ad2ae6c1f06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8aa2ebdb-a391-45f2-a8b4-8d99f94e0cd0",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** As a group, nominate the idea you've discussed that you think would be the most interesting to everyone else in the class. Start to sketch out that idea on a poster. Make a visual representation of your ideas."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8aa2ebdb-a391-45f2-a8b4-8d99f94e0cd0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1108ecf7-4bcd-4f5f-af15-ad2ae6c1f06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5d3a7783-a496-49be-8432-930fe5ec8be2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Share:** Do a \"Gallery Walk\" or a whip around so that each student can see all of the other students' work.\r\n\r\n* Put prototype posters on the wall\r\n* Give students time to survey the various posters",
        "name": "Share Prototypes\r"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5d3a7783-a496-49be-8432-930fe5ec8be2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1108ecf7-4bcd-4f5f-af15-ad2ae6c1f06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ad426e4-abdd-451c-8fd8-0f9ada13a527",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Take this opportunity to explain the importance of bringing individual interests and perspectives to this course. From day one, students should be thinking about how to apply the principles they learn to their own lives, and hopefully they will be excited to do so.\r\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ad426e4-abdd-451c-8fd8-0f9ada13a527",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8694fa92-3eae-4902-87ad-e6d1d59f59bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f92e7c72-8dbe-4362-ae13-44d9f096357f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "- We are just starting this class, but you all bring passions and knowledge about things you care about. And whatever those things are it is likely (if not inevitable) that they involve computing technology in some way.\r\n- Everyone is unique, but we’re all in this together.",
        "name": "Welcome students to the course\r",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f92e7c72-8dbe-4362-ae13-44d9f096357f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8694fa92-3eae-4902-87ad-e6d1d59f59bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34b83557-8f77-4295-98ab-1f16dcbac194",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Play [Computer Science is Changing Everything](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QvyTEx1wyOY)",
        "progression_name": "Computer Science is Changing Everything"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34b83557-8f77-4295-98ab-1f16dcbac194",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8694fa92-3eae-4902-87ad-e6d1d59f59bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cef75de5-045f-4203-bd87-1c2c1df9d075",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  **Discuss:** *What are you excited to learn about in this course?*\n\n**Discussion Goal:** Students can discuss in small groups and then share out with the whole class. It's ok if at this point students don't have an answer to this prompt. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cef75de5-045f-4203-bd87-1c2c1df9d075",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8694fa92-3eae-4902-87ad-e6d1d59f59bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cafc7206-4006-43cf-9ca4-db441a1e7a6a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "---"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cafc7206-4006-43cf-9ca4-db441a1e7a6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8694fa92-3eae-4902-87ad-e6d1d59f59bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62fc9e59-e691-42ae-9d96-58ebf575a957",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "*Check For Understanding Question(s) and solutions can be found in each lesson on Code Studio. These questions can be used for an exit ticket.*\r\n\r\nComputer science has changed the way we communicate with each other, make art and movies, grow food, and even treat illnesses. Everyone can learn computer science and make a difference.\r\n\r\nStill, we understand that taking a computer science course can be difficult at first. Here are a few student quotes describing their strategies and tips for taking this course. Please read the quotes carefully and respond to the prompt below.\r\n\r\n**Question:** What are your own strategies and insights about how to learn best? And, how are they similar or different to the ones that you just heard about from other students?",
        "name": "Assessment: Check For Understanding\r",
        "progression_name": "Check For Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62fc9e59-e691-42ae-9d96-58ebf575a957",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8694fa92-3eae-4902-87ad-e6d1d59f59bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cab693d0-324b-4b08-84b0-d28cec6636a5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Have your students complete these questions independently some time before starting this unit. ",
        "progression_name": "The Pre-Unit Pulse",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Pre-Unit Pulse Questions:** The answers to these questions can provide insights into the preferences, strengths, and motivations of your students. If you'd like to adapt the questions or add in your own, you may want to make a copy of [this Google Form](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1UU9O-FJPNZcKQfDBabXBGW3rjCoRrrtgVPvVW8P705U/copy) which already has the existing questions populated.\n\nFor more tips and ideas of how to use these questions, check out the [CSP Guide to Pre-Unit Pulse Questions](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1jx43dakN6og2n7iey_uQrNwcpMHQRbv1RCPZFHpQdYw/edit?usp=sharing)"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cab693d0-324b-4b08-84b0-d28cec6636a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ea796b3a-a153-4a3d-8be5-fec407265b23"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7109d0f0-96b6-4935-853e-5180832f6a3f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Unit 3 Journal ([Digital](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/11bPD5axn1Xjql5050aLfUSYy-G2UJWhQ-nV6nskne_E/edit?usp=share_link)/[Interactive Notebook](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1P8Iz5X_NiTUx8fBJGtKcQPQfGaR9O9ujIWBJp_H6WtQ/edit?usp=drive_link\t\n)) or have students set up this unit's section in their CSP journals.",
        "name": "Preview the Unit",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Journaling:** Journaling can take many different forms, but in general, it’s a tool for individual processing and reflection in a form that can be revisited as students develop their skills and understandings. The medium used for journaling can vary depending on classroom needs. \n\nFor more guidance and examples, check out the [**CSP Guide to Journaling**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1d9Lq_-K5vNi8osaI-VNVqcgbb-aDr4BJtHADe-MRoKU/edit?usp=sharing)\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7109d0f0-96b6-4935-853e-5180832f6a3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "534e630d-db3f-4ea2-8fb0-9942614dff9d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5d227722-6716-40eb-aad4-3b1ff34081d1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've learned how to represent information digitally and how the Internet works, it's time to think about creating digital content for others to interact with.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5d227722-6716-40eb-aad4-3b1ff34081d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "534e630d-db3f-4ea2-8fb0-9942614dff9d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e42a9986-2724-4d83-8ecf-f92de3d112a0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  **Discuss:** *What are apps? How do we interact with them? What kinds of things do apps do?*\n\n**Discussion Goal:** Apps are designed to solve a problem by providing a service\n\n* Entertainment: images, movies, tv shows, etc.\n* Social: social media, chatting\n* Commerce: marketplaces\n* Finding Information: search engines, maps\n \n Apps have screens or *User Interfaces* to display and collect information to and from users\n\n* These things collect information: Buttons, sliders, text boxes, etc.\n* This is the type of information that can be displayed: Sounds, images, video, text\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e42a9986-2724-4d83-8ecf-f92de3d112a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "534e630d-db3f-4ea2-8fb0-9942614dff9d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1691c6f-2040-4a7b-9466-389d7aaa3bd7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we are going to be looking at some sample apps to explore their purpose and function.",
        "name": "App Exploration",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson is an introduction to a unit that introduces programming through the broader context of designing apps that help people. This lesson is designed to build excitement about the skills that students will develop throughout the unit and get them thinking early about the ways programming can help others. This lesson also establishes shared vocabulary for talking about apps in a generic sense, for example its inputs, outputs, and overall user interface."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1691c6f-2040-4a7b-9466-389d7aaa3bd7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f53f710-716d-4659-842a-a3a3e901898e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Group:** Organize students in groups of two. Students should navigate to the App Exploration starting on level 1. \n\n**Levels 4-8:** There are five different sample apps. Depending on time, students can explore 3-5 different apps."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f53f710-716d-4659-842a-a3a3e901898e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f973330-f729-4e79-9a9c-0dfbc2bb4df2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n**App #1: Water Conservation Tips**\n\n* The user clicks through the different screens and makes choices about the correct ways to conserve water. When the user makes choices, images or sounds are played to indicate if a choice is the right one. \n* The purpose of the app is to teach people about ways to conserve water. \n* The target audience is young people who are interested in the topic\n\n**App #2: Bird Quiz**\n\n* The user answers trivia questions about birds. If the answer is correct, they go to the next question. If wrong, they are taken to a losing screen, and then start the quiz again. If three questions are answered correctly the user is taken to the winning screen.\n* The purpose of the app is to teach users different facts about birds\n* The target audience is anyone interested in birds\n\n**App #3: Hamilton Township**\n\n* The user clicks through a few different screens to learn about township efforts to beautify the town\n* The purpose of the app is to educate users on steps the town is taking to improve the town\n* The target audience is people who live in Hamilton Township\n\n**App #4: Four Square Instructions**\n\n* The user navigates through different screens that explain how to play four square. On one screen, the user is quizzed on how many people are needed to play four square. \n* The purpose of the app is to instruct the user on the rules of four square.\n* The target audience is people who would like to play four square.\n\n**App #5: Monarch Butterflies**\n\n* The user clicks on different screens and pictures of monarch butterflies to explore the life stages of the butterfly\n* The purpose of the app is to educate users on the life cycle of the monarch butterfly\n* The target audience is young people, most likely children or teenagers who are interested in life science and butterflies\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f973330-f729-4e79-9a9c-0dfbc2bb4df2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53aa9ec8-8b9c-42e7-8a3a-bf10cc39ef1a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "App Exploration"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53aa9ec8-8b9c-42e7-8a3a-bf10cc39ef1a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97178de9-0a58-44df-a58e-ab09e14a5b76",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  **Discuss:** *With a partner, discuss the following and note down in your journal:*\n* *How does the user interact with the app?*\n* *What is the overall purpose of this app?*\n* *Who is the target audience?*\n\n**Share Out:** As a class, discuss student answers to the discussion questions.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the **Retrieve-Pair-Share** strategy to facilitate this discussion by directing students to write down their thoughts and ideas first, then pair up students to compare their answers and expand their notes. This allows time for individual processing and idea generation before sharing thoughts with others. Ask students to share the ideas they discussed with their partners as a class.\n\nFor more ideas on how to adapt this strategy to fit the needs of your class, check out the [**CSP Guide to Classroom Discussions**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vTJpyZllcSG5T5aFfSuPs9CByoejkEMnh4U_MMMOr9g/edit?usp=sharing)\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97178de9-0a58-44df-a58e-ab09e14a5b76",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5208f1f7-f203-4b35-a063-f62d9c0d719a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In developing apps, which are a kind of computing innovation, it's important to understand both their purpose and function. The purpose helps the developer focus on creating an app with specific goals in mind. \n\nNow let's dig in to how the user interacts with the apps.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5208f1f7-f203-4b35-a063-f62d9c0d719a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e95c7b3-6f85-4775-a5df-e5b00aadfd2f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display:** How Computers Work [r how_computers_work_-_what_makes_a_computer_a_computer/csp-dev-2027/unversioned]",
        "name": "Input & Output",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Videos are used throughout the curriculum to spark discussions, supplement key concepts with additional explanations and examples, and expose students to the various roles and backgrounds of individuals in computer science.\n\nWhile interacting with the video, turn on closed captioning so students can also read along as they watch.\n\nTo encourage active engagement and reflection, use one or more of the strategies discussed in the [**Guide to Curriculum Videos**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1f8IkB75jpoFHg48FAtC8Kz1pD-5P7DKIGOytJM6o2oM/edit?usp=sharing)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e95c7b3-6f85-4775-a5df-e5b00aadfd2f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85d3906f-7b38-4501-852e-0de4301eddee",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The video explained *Input*, *Storage*, *Processing*, and *Output*. Today we are going to focus on input and output. An app on a phone gets input in many different ways from a user or from another program and displays (or in the case of sound: plays) output. The output of a program is usually based on inputs or the initial way the program was set up. For example, you could program an app to play a song as soon as it runs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85d3906f-7b38-4501-852e-0de4301eddee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8734b625-7da9-4792-b94f-e902ff498a1c",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  **Levels 9-13:** With your partner, take another look at the sample apps you explored before by navigating to the App Investigations starting at level 6. Consider what the inputs and outputs are for the apps. Note these down in your journal.",
        "name": "App Investigation",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "After students are finished writing in their journals, discuss as a class or collect the journals to review student answers. \n\n* An input could be a user clicking a button or tapping the screen\n* An output could be an image displayed or a sound played\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8734b625-7da9-4792-b94f-e902ff498a1c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e21db7ae-35fe-408b-9ab8-8727c1d4d3db",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "App Investigation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e21db7ae-35fe-408b-9ab8-8727c1d4d3db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "454826e5-ea82-4510-87ea-ba400b813326"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91f790c0-3553-44ee-b7b0-b0e863df46f7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Apps have unique purposes that appeal to different types of users. Apps take in input from users and output information in various ways. Users interact with apps through the *user interface*.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91f790c0-3553-44ee-b7b0-b0e863df46f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dab9c05c-24d2-4b70-bd92-aca1d90cdac6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a9a09f1-b40b-4e6f-a192-769fa426bffe",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  **Discuss or Write:** *Focus on an app from this level and explain its purpose. Give an example of who a potential user of such an app might be.*\n\nDepending on the amount of time that you have available in class, you can choose to either have a whole-class discussion of the prompt or a writing opportunity for students to practice their skills for the new Create PT Task.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a9a09f1-b40b-4e6f-a192-769fa426bffe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dab9c05c-24d2-4b70-bd92-aca1d90cdac6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "415fc295-5f05-4585-aae2-172eaea60cb4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "<b>Create Performance Task Written Response Practice</b>\n\nIn order to help prepare students for the new component of the AP Create Performance Task, there will be several opportunities throughout the curriculum to practice creating written responses aligned with the learning objectives that might be assessed on the exam.\n\n</br>\n\nSample Prompt: <i> Focus on an app from this level and explain its purpose. Give an example of who a potential user of such an app might be.</i>\n\n\n<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/11bPD5axn1Xjql5050aLfUSYy-G2UJWhQ-nV6nskne_E/edit#slide=id.g22e15123a99_0_388\">Unit Journal Template</a>\n\n<b>Sample Response for Instructors</b> (<i>coming soon...</i>)"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "415fc295-5f05-4585-aae2-172eaea60cb4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dab9c05c-24d2-4b70-bd92-aca1d90cdac6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e2e23026-8057-4707-a65b-a8061110850c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Discussion / Writing Goal:** Have students share their thoughts with a partner. Then, bring the class back together and discuss a few examples. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e2e23026-8057-4707-a65b-a8061110850c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dab9c05c-24d2-4b70-bd92-aca1d90cdac6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79692ddc-cb96-4466-9698-4df8e91bad56",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Journal** Add to your journal the following terms along with your own definitions:\n\n* User Interface\n* Input\n* Output\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Vocabulary Canvas:** You may choose to add your own examples to the slides. The image below shows an example of a Vocabulary Canvas. Also, you can find definitions and graphics for the vocabulary words in the hidden slides at the end of the lesson slides if you'd like to use those with your students.\n\n\n![](https://images.code.org/41e2d57a9754d5eaf279b3646d50a711-Screenshot 2023-04-14 at 3.39.47 PM.png)\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79692ddc-cb96-4466-9698-4df8e91bad56",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dab9c05c-24d2-4b70-bd92-aca1d90cdac6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7d62d1b-8a7f-482d-b4dd-ffae33843ee2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "---"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7d62d1b-8a7f-482d-b4dd-ffae33843ee2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dab9c05c-24d2-4b70-bd92-aca1d90cdac6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9636ecbd-a2b1-4506-a561-e7af159f11ec",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "*Check For Understanding Question(s) and solutions can be found in each lesson on Code Studio. These questions can be used for an exit ticket.*\r\n\r\n**Question:** Match term with the example.",
        "name": "Assessment: Check For Understanding\r",
        "progression_name": "Check For Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9636ecbd-a2b1-4506-a561-e7af159f11ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dab9c05c-24d2-4b70-bd92-aca1d90cdac6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a31b106f-124b-4e27-ba78-b45d2ec72e6c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Unit 1 Journal ([Digital](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1nCwU7a83mgtEIKId1Fw-JzsZkHicWN12WeCTDMJkw68/edit?usp=share_link)/[Interactive Notebook](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1hydmwrk4buHxg1gW-4TvVKMkIDvru8FTRuk53N8T8es/edit?usp=drive_link)) or have students set up this unit's section in their CSP journals.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Journaling:** Journaling can take many different forms, but in general, it’s a tool for individual processing and reflection in a form that can be revisited as students develop their skills and understandings. The medium used for journaling can vary depending on classroom needs. \n\nFor more guidance and examples, check out the [**CSP Guide to Journaling**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1d9Lq_-K5vNi8osaI-VNVqcgbb-aDr4BJtHADe-MRoKU/edit?usp=sharing)\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a31b106f-124b-4e27-ba78-b45d2ec72e6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6bdff9e-67aa-4c76-835a-9099a8ff762c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79d5c556-0108-431a-81a2-b470a6cfd808",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A lot of people think that computer science is the study of computers, like the phone in your pocket or the computer on your desk. As we'll see this year, computer science actually has a lot more to do with information.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79d5c556-0108-431a-81a2-b470a6cfd808",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6bdff9e-67aa-4c76-835a-9099a8ff762c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e2eacb4-043e-42a9-936f-671d31dde148",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  **Discuss:** What is your definition of information?\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Reminder:** A slide icon ![](https://curriculum.code.org/media/uploads/singleslide.svg)  indicates there is a corresponding slide in the unit slide deck. "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e2eacb4-043e-42a9-936f-671d31dde148",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6bdff9e-67aa-4c76-835a-9099a8ff762c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2e243431-4a2c-425b-8177-bbf881bce1a7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Have students silently write their own definition, then share with a partner, and finally have some volunteers share with the room.\n\n**Discussion Goal:** More important than writing a formal definition of information is getting students discussing the term and its diverse forms. If students struggle with defining \"information,\" modify the prompt by asking them to provide examples of information in their world such as texts, books, videos, music, conversations, etc.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the **Retrieve-Pair-Share** strategy to facilitate this discussion by directing students to write down their thoughts and ideas first, then pair up students to compare their answers and expand their notes. This allows time for individual processing and idea generation before sharing thoughts with others. Ask students to share the ideas they discussed with their partners as a class.\n\nFor more ideas on how to adapt this strategy to fit the needs of your class, check out the [**CSP Guide to Classroom Discussions**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vTJpyZllcSG5T5aFfSuPs9CByoejkEMnh4U_MMMOr9g/edit?usp=sharing)"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2e243431-4a2c-425b-8177-bbf881bce1a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6bdff9e-67aa-4c76-835a-9099a8ff762c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68e7d1eb-dec7-401a-b616-62d1bf9e792c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "* There are many ways we can think about the word “information,” but one possible definition we’ll explore today is that information is the answer to a question. \r\n* We're going to investigate what it takes to send that information back and forth to one another.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68e7d1eb-dec7-401a-b616-62d1bf9e792c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6bdff9e-67aa-4c76-835a-9099a8ff762c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b1d8a7a-9225-47ef-84e9-754e9e372350",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Group:** Place students in groups of two.\r\n\r\n\r\n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Materials for students to build their binary message devices (straws, scissors, etc.).",
        "name": "Information Sending Devices\r"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b1d8a7a-9225-47ef-84e9-754e9e372350",
        "lesson_activity.key": "641c577f-2e89-4162-a80e-c858e10821f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04f17253-b1ef-47cc-958e-123441214336",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today you are going to build your own device for sending and receiving messages which you will use to answer questions that you write.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04f17253-b1ef-47cc-958e-123441214336",
        "lesson_activity.key": "641c577f-2e89-4162-a80e-c858e10821f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe566e15-b3a6-4a88-a975-1c26a0b34540",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  **Journal:** Write down a question that has two possible answers.",
        "name": "Challenge #1:\r"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe566e15-b3a6-4a88-a975-1c26a0b34540",
        "lesson_activity.key": "641c577f-2e89-4162-a80e-c858e10821f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e03264af-0a70-4646-89c1-d37a692a7ffd",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Build a device out of classroom supplies to communicate the answer to your question. \r\n\r\n* Rules: \r\n\t* No projectiles.\r\n\t* No language can be used.\r\n\t\t* For example: If my question is \"Is your favorite color blue or green?\" I can't write the words blue and green on my device.  \t \r\n\r\n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** After students have completed their devices, choose a few groups to demonstrate their device in action. Students stand on opposite sides of the classroom. One student asks the agreed upon question. The other student uses the device to communicate the answer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e03264af-0a70-4646-89c1-d37a692a7ffd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "641c577f-2e89-4162-a80e-c858e10821f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1db44e5f-691d-40ef-84fa-9607540ddde2",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Journal:** Modify the answer to your question so there are now four possible answers. \r\n\r\n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Update your device to communicate one of four possible answers to your question. \r\n\r\n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Again, choose groups to demonstrate their updated devices.",
        "name": "Challenge #2:\r"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1db44e5f-691d-40ef-84fa-9607540ddde2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "641c577f-2e89-4162-a80e-c858e10821f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55a0c7db-4c82-4874-9875-01a507740aec",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Journal:** Modify the answers to your question so there are now eight possible choices. \r\n\r\n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Update your device to communicate one of eight possible answers to your question.\r\n\r\n* Consider: \r\n\r\n\t* Should you modify your device?\r\n\t* Can you use it in a different way?\r\n\t* Should you make a new device?\r\n\r\n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Demo:** Do a final demonstration of devices before ending the activity.",
        "name": "Challenge #3:\r",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Challenge 1 - Question with 2 Answers:** In this activity, avoid the urge to give students a pre-written question or answers without allowing them time to struggle with the challenge. Encourage students to write down in their journal how their device works. \n\n* Example Question: Do you like strawberry or vanilla ice cream?\n* Answer: strawberry (move my pencil up and down), vanilla (move my pencil side to side)\n\n**Challenge 2 - Question with 4 Answers:** Some students may add new ways of answering a question. Others may notice that they can reuse their previous responses, sending those responses in sequences that produce new messages. Avoid the urge to tell them to reuse the device, instead allowing them to explore their own ideas.\n\n**Challenge 3 - Question with 8 or More Answers:** At this point, students may start to realize that the way their device communicates information is not practical and could not be scaled up if there were for example, a thousand or a million possible answers.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55a0c7db-4c82-4874-9875-01a507740aec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "641c577f-2e89-4162-a80e-c858e10821f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a35b1e00-98f5-4c16-8c01-93d2a2307083",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's wrap up by discussing how you designed your devices and what that means about the questions you can ask and the messages you can send.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a35b1e00-98f5-4c16-8c01-93d2a2307083",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c0c6036-e3d2-407e-b33b-8924dffe1f33"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b535071-b561-4e23-b72e-8c2386166115",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>  **Discuss:** *Think back to your simple two-option device from Challenge #1. Instead of changing your device and adding more options every time you added more answers, how could you simply modify the way you use your device with only two options?*\n\n**Discussion Goal:** Focus the discussion on why some groups created devices using unique options for each additional message, while other groups devised plans in which they reused the same device to create new combinations of the original two options. \n\nUltimately, groups begin to notice that, for example: instead of bending a straw in four different directions, they could simply bend the straw the same two ways multiple times.\n\n* Sample Question: Do you like strawberry, chocolate, vanilla, or peanut butter ice cream?\n* Answers: strawberry (bend straw forward once), chocolate (bend straw forward twice), vanilla (bend straw backwards once), peanut butter (bend straw backwards twice)\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b535071-b561-4e23-b72e-8c2386166115",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c0c6036-e3d2-407e-b33b-8924dffe1f33"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5a88a90-9a6b-49ab-8cf1-184b702967fb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "---"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5a88a90-9a6b-49ab-8cf1-184b702967fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c0c6036-e3d2-407e-b33b-8924dffe1f33"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92391f5e-91fe-45d4-ac3e-a7cf21a46c0b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "*Check For Understanding Question(s) and solutions can be found in each lesson on Code Studio. These questions can be used for an exit ticket.*\r\n\r\n**Question:** Recall when you built your information sending device. Why did we decide to send a message as a sequence of two options rather than modifying our devices to represent more options? \r\n\r\nFor example: \r\nModifications with two options \r\n\r\n* bend straw forward once\r\n* bend straw backwards once\r\n* bend straw forwards twice\r\n* bend straw backwards twice\r\n\r\nvs.\r\n\r\nFour options\r\n\r\n* bend straw forward\r\n* bend straw backwards\r\n* bend straw to the left\r\n* bend straw to the right",
        "name": "Assessment: Check For Understanding\r",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92391f5e-91fe-45d4-ac3e-a7cf21a46c0b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c0c6036-e3d2-407e-b33b-8924dffe1f33"
      }
    }
  ],
